WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.990
Our class kept a reading diary for a term.

2
00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:06.240
Students who read more often scored

3
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.000
higher in the reading test.

4
00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:10.730
Could we predict a new student's

5
00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:12.110
mark from their reading hours?

6
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:16.020
Today: how a straight line can

7
00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:19.200
predict, and when not to trust it.

8
00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.300
A model is a simple rule learned from

9
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.020
data that makes a prediction.

10
00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:27.530
The simplest model is a straight

11
00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:29.130
line through a scatter plot.

12
00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:31.600
Finding that line is called

13
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:32.800
linear regression.

14
00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:36.170
Many lines could pass through the dots.

15
00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.280
The best fit line keeps the gaps between

16
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:41.440
the dots and the line as small as

17
00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:41.800
possible.

18
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.160
The computer finds it in a moment.

19
00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.670
Here are average marks for pupils

20
00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:50.050
grouped by hours of reading each week.

21
00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:52.860
They climb steadily, about two

22
00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.800
marks for every extra hour.

23
00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:58.260
The line has a slope and a start.

24
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.360
Here the slope is two marks per hour, and

25
00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.940
the start is eight marks for no reading.

26
00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.480
So the prediction is eight

27
00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.060
plus two times the hours.

28
00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:11.540
Careful with lines.

29
00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.020
Our pupils read between zero and five

30
00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:17.600
hours, so the line only knows that range.

31
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:20.790
And a line shows a pattern, not

32
00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:22.290
a promise for one pupil.

33
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:23.920
Quick check!

34
00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:27.120
The slope is two marks per hour.

35
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:30.480
If a pupil reads three more hours a week,

36
00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.060
how many more marks does the line predict?

37
00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:35.440
Six more marks.

38
00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:38.130
Three hours times two

39
00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:39.790
marks per hour is six.

40
00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:42.830
That is what slope means: the

41
00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:44.930
change for each extra unit.

42
00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.100
In the app, open the scatter lab

43
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:49.840
and show the line of best fit.

44
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:53.560
Tap the chart to add one pupil far from

45
00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:55.700
the others and watch the line tilt.

46
00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:57.450
Your turn.

47
00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:00.680
Our pupils read up to five hours.

48
00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:03.880
Should we trust the line to predict

49
00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:05.680
the mark for forty hours a week?

50
00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.240
No, forty hours is far outside our data.

51
00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:13.430
Remember: the best fit line is a model,

52
00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:16.870
its slope is the change per unit, and it

53
00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:19.230
only works inside the data you had.

54
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.120
Try it yourself: measure the height and

55
00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.640
arm span of a few people, and lay a

56
00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:27.400
ruler where the line fits best.

57
00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:29.830
Predict someone new, then

58
00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:31.090
measure and compare.
